The cushion cut vs emerald cut diamond comparison looks simple from the outside — both are popular, both come in square and rectangular versions, both pair well with platinum and gold settings.

They are not the same diamond at all.

The cushion cut is a brilliant cut with curved, pillow-shaped facets that scatter light in every direction. The emerald cut is a step cut with long parallel facets that create a hall-of-mirrors reflection. One sparkles.

One reflects.

The difference is visible from across the room.

Price is the other story. Cushion cuts run cheaper than emerald cuts at every carat weight — sometimes significantly so — because the emerald cut demands higher clarity and color grades to look good in its open table.

A cushion can hide VS2 inclusions. An emerald cut cannot.

This guide covers both shapes completely: geometry, certified market pricing from 1ct to 5ct, the two cushion cut variants you need to know about, clarity floors, color minimums, and exactly which buyer should choose which shape.

---

**TL;DR — 5 Differences That Actually Matter**

- **1. Light performance** — Cushion: brilliant cut, scattered sparkle. Emerald: step cut, hall-of-mirrors. They look nothing alike face-up.
- **2. Clarity floor** — Cushion hides inclusions at VS2. Emerald needs VS1 minimum — open table shows everything.
- **3. Color tolerance** — Cushion is safe at H in white gold. Emerald shows warmth at H — G is the minimum in platinum.
- **4. Price** — Cushion wins at every carat. Natural 1ct GIA G-VS1 cushion from [$1,891](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/23740863?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). Natural 1ct GIA D-VS1 emerald from [$3,140](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29333987?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational).
- **5. The cushion divide** — Two variants exist: modified brilliant (crushed ice pattern) and cushion brilliant (chunky flashes). Know which one you're buying before you commit.

---

## Expert Summary

The cushion cut is one of the oldest diamond shapes. Its rounded corners and curved facets were designed to maximize brilliance in candlelight — a job the modern cushion still does well. Two variants exist in the market today: cushion modified brilliant and cushion brilliant.

They look and perform differently.

The emerald cut is an Art Deco classic. Its long step-cut facets create a window into the stone. There are no scattered facets to hide inclusions.

There are no fragmented light patterns to disguise warm body color. The emerald cut shows everything — which means buyers must spend more on clarity and color to achieve the same appearance standards.

At natural 1ct, a GIA G-VS1 cushion starts at [$1,891](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/23740863?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). A GIA D-VS1 emerald starts at [$3,140](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29333987?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). That gap compounds with size.

At 2ct natural, a GIA cushion starts under $14,000. A comparable GIA emerald starts over $24,000.

Lab-grown pricing converges at 2ct — both shapes land near $4,700–$4,800 IGI D-IF/D-FL. Above 2ct in lab, cushion continues to offer better value because buyers don't need to spend up on clarity.

A 3ct lab cushion at GIA D-VVS1 looks eye-clean and white at [$5,962](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/26315453?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). Getting a 3ct lab emerald cut to match that visual standard costs [$9,235](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28885081?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) — a $3,273 step-cut premium even in lab.

---

## Shape Geometry: How Cushion and Emerald Cut Are Built

The cushion cut is derived from the old mine cut, the predecessor to today's round brilliant. It has a square or rectangular outline with rounded corners. The cushion modified brilliant typically has an extra row of facets under the girdle, creating a sparkle pattern that looks distinctive — sometimes described as "crushed ice." The cushion brilliant has fewer facets under the girdle, creating larger, chunkier flashes of light.

Both cushion variants are brilliant cuts. Light scatters across the stone in all directions. Inclusions are fragmented and dispersed.

Color is broken into sparkle. The cushion is forgiving in the same way an oval is forgiving.

The emerald cut is a step cut. Its rectangular body has cropped corners and long, parallel facets arranged in horizontal rows. The table is wide and flat.

There are no curved facets and no fragmented light.

Light enters through the large table, bounces between the parallel step facets, and exits in long, slow reflective planes. This is the hall-of-mirrors effect that makes the emerald cut look unlike any brilliant cut. Inclusions and color are fully visible.

