## The Step-Cut Premium

The emerald cut commands a price premium over the cushion cut. At 1ct, a comparable cushion runs roughly 40% cheaper. At 3ct, nearly 50% cheaper.

This premium exists for a structural reason: emerald cut buyers must purchase higher clarity and color grades. This is The Step-Cut Premium.

**Why emerald cut buyers pay more on clarity:**

A cushion's brilliant facets scatter light so aggressively that VS2 is almost always eye-clean. Many cushion buyers successfully buy SI1 if the inclusion is positioned near the girdle. The emerald cut's open step-cut table is a window.

VS2 in an emerald cut sometimes shows to the naked eye — the safe floor is VS1.

Moving from VS2 to VS1 at 2ct natural adds approximately $2,000–$5,000 depending on the stone. That one clarity grade accounts for a significant portion of the total price gap between comparable cushion and emerald cut diamonds.

At 3ct, the step-cut premium becomes stark: a [3ct GIA G-VVS2 natural cushion](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28961123?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) costs [$32,692](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28961123?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational), while a [3ct GIA D-VVS1 natural emerald](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/15813280?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) costs [$63,574](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/15813280?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). The emerald is nearly twice the price. Part of that gap is the grade floor the emerald cut demands — the cushion's G-VVS2 is eye-clean; getting the emerald to look as good requires D-VVS1.

**Why emerald cut buyers pay more on color:**

A cushion cut's 58–64 facets break body color into scintillation. H-color in a cushion looks white in platinum. The emerald cut's long unbroken step-cut facets reflect body color as warmth.

G is the comfortable minimum in platinum for an emerald cut. H in a step-cut stone reads slightly warm.

Moving from H to G adds cost at every carat weight. Combined with the clarity premium, the total grade premium for emerald cut buyers is real and systematic.

> **Farzana says:** "The Step-Cut Premium is not about the shape costing more. It is about the emerald cut demanding higher grades to look the same. A G-VS1 cushion and a G-VS1 emerald look completely different — but to get the emerald cut to look like the cushion does at G-VS2, you must pay for VS1. That extra grade is the premium. It is not a rip-off. It is physics."

