## Settings: What Works Best for Each Shape

Both shapes work in all major setting styles. Each has settings that show it best.

**Cushion cut settings:**

The cushion pairs beautifully with halo settings. The curved outline of the halo mirrors the cushion's rounded corners and amplifies the face-up size significantly. A 1.5ct cushion in a halo reads like a 2ct+ stone.

Pavé bands complement the cushion's sparkle perfectly. The tiny diamonds along the band contribute to the overall scintillation pattern without competing with the center stone. The result is a ring that looks brilliant in all lighting conditions.

Solitaires work well when the cushion is large enough to stand alone — 1.5ct and above. A clean four-prong solitaire shows off the cushion's shape without distraction. Vintage and milgrain settings are a natural match given the cushion's historical roots in Victorian-era diamond cutting.

**Emerald cut settings:**

The solitaire is the definitive emerald cut setting. A clean, minimal four-prong or six-prong solitaire lets the step-cut's long reflections dominate the face. Anything that competes with the table detracts from the hall-of-mirrors effect.

Bezel settings are the most protective option for the emerald cut's four cropped corners. A full bezel also creates a modern, architectural look that complements the stone's rectangular geometry.

Three-stone settings with baguette or trapezoid side stones echo the emerald cut's step-cut character. The side stones are from the same family — angular, reflective, calm. This is the most harmonious pairing for an emerald cut center.

